        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#head-to-head" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Comparison Table</a>
          <a href="#portable" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Case for Portable</a>
          <a href="#standby" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Case for Standby</a>
          <a href="#transfer-switch" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Transfer Switch</a>
          <a href="#choosing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Which Should You Buy?</a>
          <a href="#fuel" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Fuel and Runtime</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A standby generator is a permanently installed appliance that senses the outage and starts itself within seconds, whether or not anyone is home. A portable is a machine in the garage that does nothing until somebody rolls it out and starts it. Almost every other difference follows from that one.
        </p>

        <div id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Portable vs Standby at a Glance
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Portable</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Standby</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Starts automatically</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, manual every time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, within seconds</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Works when nobody is home</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical output</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,500-9,500 watts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10,000-26,000 watts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fuel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gasoline, propane, or dual fuel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Natural gas or large propane tank</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refuelling during an outage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 8-12 hours, by hand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None; runs off the gas main</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Installation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None, or a transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Permanent, electrician and often permits</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Runs central air conditioning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Noise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Loud, except inverter models</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate, further from the house</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Storage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Takes up garage space</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Outside on a pad</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Relative cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High, plus installation</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Question That Actually Decides It</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">How long are your outages, and will you be there?</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Short outages a few times a year while you are home: a portable is sound value. Multi-day outages, a medically necessary load, a sump pump protecting a finished basement, or a house that is regularly empty: the automatic start is the whole point and a portable cannot provide it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="portable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Case for a Portable Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable generator is by far the more common choice, and for most households facing occasional short outages it is the right one. The value proposition is straightforward: for a fraction of the cost of a standby installation you get enough power to keep the critical loads alive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Portable Genuinely Does Well</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Cost. A capable dual-fuel portable costs a small fraction of an installed standby system.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Flexibility. It powers a campsite, a job site or a neighbour’s house as easily as your own.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>No installation required to start using it, if you are running extension cords.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Fuel choice. Dual and tri-fuel models run on gasoline, propane or natural gas depending on what you can get.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Sizing headroom. A 7,500W portable covers the essential loads of most homes comfortably.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What You Are Accepting</h3>

          <p>
            The manual start is the obvious limitation, and it is a bigger one than it sounds. Outages tend to arrive with the weather that caused them, which means going outside in a storm, in the dark, to roll out a heavy machine and start it. If the outage begins while you are at work or away for a weekend, the freezer thaws and the sump pump sits idle regardless of what is in the garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Refuelling is the second. A portable running a meaningful load burns through fuel in eight to twelve hours, which means storing fuel safely, rotating it so it stays fresh, and getting up in the night to refill during a long outage. In a regional outage, filling stations are frequently closed or out of fuel precisely when you need them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is capacity. Most portables cannot start a three-ton central air conditioner, which surges to somewhere between 9,000 and 12,000 watts. If summer outages and air conditioning are your concern rather than winter heating, a portable is often the wrong tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="standby" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Case for a Standby Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standby generator is a permanently installed appliance wired into your electrical panel through an automatic transfer switch and fed from the natural gas main or a large propane tank. It monitors utility power continuously, and when the power fails it starts and transfers the house over within seconds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Standby Genuinely Does Well</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Automatic operation. No one needs to be home, awake or willing to go outside.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Unlimited runtime on natural gas, with no refuelling and no fuel storage.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Capacity to run central air conditioning, well pumps and electric ranges together.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Self-testing. It exercises itself weekly and reports faults before you need it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It is a permanent fixture rather than something taking up garage space.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What You Are Accepting</h3>

          <p>
            Cost is the obvious one, and it is not just the unit. A standby installation involves an electrician, a gas fitter, a concrete pad, an automatic transfer switch, and usually permits and inspection. The installation frequently approaches or exceeds the price of the generator itself, and it varies enormously with how far the unit sits from the panel and the gas meter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is that it is a fixed asset tied to the house. You cannot take it camping, lend it to a neighbour or take it with you when you move — though it does generally add to the property’s value and appeal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is maintenance. A standby unit is an engine that sits outside for years and runs rarely. It needs oil changes, filter changes and periodic service to be reliable when called on, and skipping that maintenance is the most common reason a standby generator fails to start during the outage it was bought for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transfer-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Part Most People Overlook: The Transfer Switch
          </h2>

          <p>
            A transfer switch connects a generator to your home’s wiring while safely isolating it from the utility grid. It is mandatory on a standby installation and strongly advisable on a portable one, and it is the single most important safety component in either system.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Never Backfeed Your Panel</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A double-ended cord into a wall outlet can kill a lineworker.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Feeding a generator into a dryer outlet or any receptacle to energise the house — sometimes called a suicide cord — pushes power back onto the utility lines. It can electrocute utility crews working to restore your power, it bypasses every circuit protection in the house, and it is illegal in most jurisdictions. Use a properly installed transfer switch.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Works With</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How It Works</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Senses the outage and switches over unattended</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Manual transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">You flip it after starting the generator</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interlock kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Panel-mounted plate; cheaper, requires an electrician</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extension cords only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No hardwired circuits; cannot run a furnace</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That last row matters more than people expect. Extension cords can run a refrigerator, a freezer and lamps, but they cannot run anything hardwired — and a furnace, a sump pump and a well pump are typically hardwired. If those are the loads you actually care about, a portable generator without a transfer switch or interlock will not power them.
          </p>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch or an interlock kit is a modest addition to a portable generator’s cost and it transforms what the machine can do. If you are buying a portable specifically for outage backup, budget for it from the start rather than discovering the limitation during the first outage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which One Should You Actually Buy?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy a Portable If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Your outages are occasional and typically last hours rather than days.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Someone is usually home and able to start and refuel it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Your essential loads are the fridge, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump and some lights.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Budget is a primary constraint, or you may move before a standby pays back.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You want a machine that can also serve camping, job sites or a workshop.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy a Standby If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Outages in your area routinely run for days rather than hours.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The house is empty for stretches, or a finished basement depends on a sump pump.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Someone in the house has a medical device or health condition that needs reliable power.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You want central air conditioning during summer outages.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>You would rather not store fuel or refuel a machine in the middle of the night.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A middle path worth knowing about: a large dual-fuel or tri-fuel portable paired with a manual transfer switch or interlock kit gets you a substantial share of the standby experience — hardwired circuits including the furnace and sump pump, and the ability to run on propane or natural gas rather than stored gasoline — at a fraction of the installed cost. What it will never give you is the automatic start, and that is the one thing that cannot be retrofitted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before committing either way, size the load properly. Our generator sizing guide includes a wattage calculator that adds your running loads and the largest single start-up surge, which is the number that determines whether a 7,500W portable is enough or whether you are genuinely in standby territory.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel, Runtime and the Multi-Day Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel is where the two types diverge most sharply during a long outage, and it is the factor people underestimate before they have lived through one.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fuel</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Used By</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Runtime</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Practical Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gasoline</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 hours per tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Degrades in months; stations close in outages</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane, 20lb tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-8 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stores indefinitely; easy to keep spares</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane, 500 gal tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Days to weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs a large tank on the property</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Natural gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unlimited</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Piped supply; no storage, no refuelling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dual or tri-fuel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexibility when one fuel is unavailable</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Dual Fuel on a Portable</h3>

          <p>
            If you are buying a portable specifically for outage backup rather than for recreation, dual fuel is worth the modest premium. Gasoline degrades within months unless treated with stabiliser and rotated, and during a regional outage the filling stations are frequently shut or empty precisely when you need them. Propane stores indefinitely, so a few spare cylinders in the garage represent guaranteed runtime that gasoline never quite does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Natural Gas Is the Standby Advantage</h3>

          <p>
            A standby unit fed from the gas main has no runtime limit and no refuelling at all. Through a five-day outage it simply runs, without anyone visiting it, carrying fuel or getting up in the night. That is a genuinely different experience from a portable that needs attention twice a day, and for households that regularly face long outages it is most of the justification for the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            The caveat is that natural gas supply is not guaranteed in every disaster. In a severe earthquake or a major infrastructure failure the gas main can be shut off as a safety measure, at which point a natural gas standby unit has no fuel at all. In seismically active regions this is a genuine argument for a propane tank on the property rather than a mains connection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding the Panel With a Double-Ended Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Feeding a generator into a dryer or wall outlet to energise the house pushes power back onto the utility lines, where it can electrocute crews working to restore your service. It also bypasses the panel’s circuit protection. It is illegal in most jurisdictions. Install a transfer switch or an interlock kit instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Portable for Loads It Cannot Reach</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Furnaces, sump pumps and well pumps are typically hardwired, and no extension cord reaches them. A portable without a transfer switch or interlock can run a fridge and some lamps and nothing else. If those hardwired loads are why you are buying, budget for the switch from the start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing for Running Watts and Forgetting the Surge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Motor-driven appliances draw far more at start-up than while running — a sump pump running at 1,050 watts can surge to 2,600. Size for your total running watts plus the single largest surge, not the running total alone, or the generator will stall the first time the pump kicks in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Generator in a Garage or Near Windows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carbon monoxide from a portable generator kills people every year, and an open garage door is not adequate ventilation. Run it well away from the house with the exhaust directed away from doors, windows and vents, and fit working CO alarms indoors regardless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting a Standby Unit Go Unmaintained</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standby generator is an engine that sits outside for years and runs rarely, and the most common reason one fails during an outage is skipped servicing. Keep up with oil changes, filter changes and the scheduled service, and confirm the weekly self-test is actually running.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a standby generator better than a portable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For long or unattended outages, yes — it starts itself within seconds, runs off the gas main with no refuelling, and has the capacity for central air conditioning. For occasional short outages while someone is home, a portable delivers most of the practical benefit at a fraction of the cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable generator power a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not the whole house, but it can power the essentials. A 7,500W portable connected through a transfer switch typically runs a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump and lighting comfortably. What it generally cannot do is start a three-ton central air conditioner, which surges to 9,000-12,000 watts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a portable generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need one for any hardwired load — a furnace, sump pump or well pump cannot be reached by extension cord. A manual transfer switch or an interlock kit is a modest addition to the cost and dramatically expands what the generator can do. Never backfeed the panel through a wall outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a portable generator run continuously?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically eight to twelve hours on a tank under load, after which it needs refuelling by hand. Most manufacturers also recommend shutting down periodically to check oil and let the unit cool. That refuelling cycle is the practical limit on portables during multi-day outages.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a standby generator cost to install?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The installation frequently approaches or exceeds the price of the unit itself, because it involves an electrician, a gas connection, a concrete pad, an automatic transfer switch, and usually permits and inspection. Cost varies substantially with how far the unit sits from the electrical panel and the gas meter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a standby generator run my air conditioning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. Standby units generally start at around 10,000 watts and commonly run to 22,000 or more, which is enough to handle the 9,000 to 12,000 watt surge of a three-ton central air conditioner alongside other household loads. This is one of the clearest advantages over a portable.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

