        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The decision is not really about the machines. It is about whether backup power has to work when nobody is there to make it work. A standby generator starts itself in seconds and runs off the gas main indefinitely. A portable does nothing until someone rolls it out, starts it, and comes back to refuel it every eight to twelve hours.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For occasional short outages in a house that is usually occupied, a portable — ideally dual-fuel, and paired with a manual transfer switch or interlock so it can reach the furnace and sump pump — is excellent value and covers the loads that matter. For multi-day outages, an empty house, a finished basement on a sump pump, a medical need, or summer outages where you want air conditioning, the automatic start is the product and a portable cannot substitute for it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever way you lean, size it before you shop. Add your running watts, add the single largest start-up surge, and add about twenty percent of headroom. That number tells you whether you are looking at a 7,500 watt portable or genuinely need a standby unit, and it prevents the most expensive mistake in this category — buying a generator that stalls the moment the sump pump starts.
          </p>
